[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon/master/docs/branding/banner.jpg[/img][/center]
[center][size=6][b]EVENT HORIZON[/b][/size][/center]
[center][b]Build a collection that arrives the way you built it. Then keep it that way.[/b][/center]

A Vortex extension in two halves. [b]Curators[/b] get a workbench that sees the whole profile at once, reads what every mod requires, installs what is missing, and packs the exact working setup into a hash-verified package. [b]Players[/b] get an installer that rebuilds that setup one mod at a time, proves every archive matches, pins the curator's load order, and says so the moment anything drifts.

[quote][b]A Vortex Collection:[/b] "Here are the mods. Install them."
[b]Event Horizon:[/b] "Here is the exact state that worked. Rebuild it, and prove it matches."[/quote]

[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon/master/docs/screenshots/dashboard-home.png[/img][/center]


[size=5][b]For players[/b][/size]

[b]Install a collection.[/b] Drop a .ehcoll file. The plan is shown before anything is written: what you already have, what installs silently, what needs you, what can no longer be downloaded. Your game setup is checked first — an unmanaged game, a never-started game, a file from a different store's copy, an install under Program Files — and the install stops with the steps to fix it, not an hour later.

[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon/master/docs/screenshots/preview.png[/img][/center]

[b]One mod at a time.[/b] Vortex loses files when mods install at once, so this never does. Every archive is hashed against the manifest and reported as [b]matches[/b], [b]differs[/b], [b]damaged[/b] or [b]unknown[/b]. The curator's FOMOD answers are replayed; you choose once whether to watch them or let them apply.

[b]The load order is the curator's, and it stays that way.[/b] The install pins the curator's order for the collection's plugins, lets LOOT place your own plugins between them, then puts the collection's plugins back in the curator's sequence. Afterwards Event Horizon keeps watching: the moment Vortex's automatic sort or a Sort click replaces that order, a notification tells you, with [b]Re-apply curator's order[/b] right on it. Home and the Doctor show the state at a glance.

[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon/master/docs/screenshots/load-order-drifted.png[/img][/center]

[b]Collection Doctor.[/b] Is this collection still what was installed? Profile, mods present, mods enabled, staged files, plugin order, ESL flags, mod rules, LOOT rules — each answered separately, and "not checked" is never shown as "fine". Every finding that can be repaired has the repair on its card, run through the same step the install used.

[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon/master/docs/screenshots/doctor.png[/img][/center]

[b]An interrupted install continues where it stopped[/b], in the same profile, even after Vortex was killed. Every ending leaves a trace under [b]My Collections[/b]: a finished install writes a receipt, a failed or cancelled one records exactly where it stopped.

[b]Linux and Steam Deck are supported targets, not tolerated ones.[/b] Event Horizon reads its own packages with its own ZIP reader, so nothing is spawned inside a Proton prefix that can fail for reasons unrelated to your files. A 950-mod collection has been installed and verified under umu/Proton.


[size=5][b]For curators[/b][/size]

[b]Curator Tools is one workbench.[/b] Your whole profile in one table: updates, manual updates, frozen mods, missing requirements, mods others depend on, duplicates, disabled, outside-Data kinds, non-Nexus archives. Views combine, a search box finds a mod, a requirement, a plugin or a provider by name, and the action bar offers only what applies to what you ticked.

[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon/master/docs/screenshots/curator-requirements.png[/img][/center]

[list]
[*][b]Requirements, read and acted on.[/b] Every mod's Nexus requirements and every plugin's masters, resolved against your pool: satisfied, installed but disabled, missing, off Nexus, DLC. Each line gets the one action that fits. Enabling a mod enables the providers it lists; disabling a provider tells you who needs it and offers to take them down too.
[*][b]Make it work.[/b] One button reads the whole chain of what a mod is missing — each requirement's own requirements too — shows the plan, lets you choose where a page ships several files, then installs in dependency order and waits for each to land. Without Nexus Premium it runs guided, opening each page in turn.
[*][b]Bulk update, one mod at a time[/b], each verified against its archive afterwards. [b]Freeze[/b] a mod at a version and be told loudly if Vortex's own update button moves it anyway. [b]Endorse[/b] with Nexus's answer read back per mod.
[*][b]Plugins:[/b] which mod ships each plugin, missing versus disabled masters, the light flag, and the regular-slot count against the 254 limit. Enable, disable and flag light from here.
[*][b]Downloads[/b] nothing was ever made from, installed on request. [b]Disk cleanup[/b] that never chooses removals for you, and separates proven old versions from files that merely share a page.
[*][b]Notes[/b] on a mod. Start one with @users and it ships in the collection, shown to installers under "From the curator".
[/list]

[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon/master/docs/screenshots/curator-install-plan.png[/img][/center]

[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon/master/docs/screenshots/curator-plugins.png[/img][/center]

[b]Build.[/b] The build asks Nexus about every mod in your collection and separates [b]file gone[/b] from [b]page gone[/b] — you are the one person who cannot notice, because your copy is already on disk. A mod that can no longer be downloaded ships as an external dependency, by hash, with a bundle, a link or instructions. Mirroring reproduces your staging folder byte for byte; a fourth FOMOD answer, "leave it out", is measured instead of guessed; two builds cannot share a version number.

[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon/master/docs/screenshots/build-form.png[/img][/center]


[size=5][b]Two things it will do that you should know about[/b][/size]

[b]1. It replaces your mod rules and LOOT userlist. It does not merge them.[/b]

Everything is backed up first, and the backup reaching disk is a hard interlock — the purge does not run without it. Merging the collection's rules into yours would produce a rule set that exists on nobody else's machine, and it fails invisibly: every file verifies, and the game still loads something different.

[b]2. Nexus Premium is recommended, not required.[/b]

Without it, large installs and "Make it work" run guided: one page opened at a time, one "Mod manager download" click from you per mod. Nothing is blocked, and you are told before it starts.


[size=5][b]Known gap[/b][/size]

File overrides are captured in every package and not yet applied on the install side. It is the last captured thing that is recorded and not used, and it is said here rather than buried because it bounds the word "deterministic".


[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Vortex 2.x.[/b] Built against vortex-api 2.6.0-beta.2 (React 18). It will [b]not[/b] run on Vortex 1.16.
[*][b]Windows, or Linux via Proton.[/b] Both supported.
[*][b]Nexus Premium[/b] recommended, not required.
[/list]


[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download the archive from this page.
[*]In Vortex, open [b]Extensions[/b] and drop the zip onto the drop zone at the bottom of the list.
[*]Restart Vortex when prompted.
[*][b]Event Horizon[/b] appears in the sidebar. There is nothing to configure.
[/list]


[size=5][b]Supported games[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Skyrim Special Edition[/b]
[*][b]Fallout 4[/b]
[*][b]Fallout 3[/b]
[*][b]Fallout New Vegas[/b]
[*][b]Starfield[/b]
[/list]

Proven on a real 950-mod Fallout 4 collection, on Windows and on Linux under Proton, and on a 1,900-mod Skyrim Special Edition profile.


[size=5][b]Source, changelog and issues[/b][/size]

Source, issue tracker, full technical documentation and a changelog written for the people who use it:
[url=https://github.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon]github.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon[/url]

Bug reports are far more useful than endorsements. If an install goes wrong, the Doctor can save every log into one zip — attach that.


[size=5][b]Credits[/b][/size]

Built by [b]DuduPhudu[/b] and [b]Bluuuk[/b]. MIT licensed.

[center][i]Vortex is a black hole. Collections, rules, FOMOD selections, conflict overrides — they all get pulled in and never come out the same on the other side. Event Horizon is the boundary that captures everything before it crosses over.[/i][/center]
